UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 7: Digital basics and data', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCount a 4 by 4 border that is all on, except the four centre cells are off. How many cells are on, how many are off, and what is the total?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on. The four centre cells are off, and 12 + 4 = 16, which matches 4 x 4. The border is a hollow square, not a filled square, because those four middle cells stay off.\n\nWATCH FOR\nA common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nOn paper, count on cells, off cells and the total for a 4 by 4 line whose every row is off, on, off, off. Name the simple shape those on cells make.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Information and Software Technology","learning_intention":"A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example.","lesson_number":7,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CT5-DAT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: On paper, count on cells, off cells and the total for a 4 by 4 line whose every row is off, on, off, off. Name the simple shape those on cells make. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using pixel on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells.. Correction: The border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating pixel on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with pixel, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about pixel with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for pixel. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for pixel. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work","The border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on"],"question":"Count a 4 by 4 border that is all on, except the four centre cells are off. How many cells are on, how many are off, and what is the total?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for pixel; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example","The border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on","Careful use of grid with clear evidence","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named grid, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name grid only, without linking it to pixel","Apply pixel to new material and name the grid used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells"],"question":"Which response best applies pixel with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on pixel. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells","The border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves pixel","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells","Name grid only, with no linked example","On paper, count on cells, off cells and the total for a 4 by 4 line whose every row is off, on, off, off"],"question":"Which independent response best applies pixel to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about pixel with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating pixel on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using pixel on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for pixel. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with pixel, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells.. Correction: The border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-information-software-technology-y9-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue learning area is Information and Software Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Computing Technology 7–10 (2022). Stage 4 may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (CT4-ADJ-01). Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. No live network attacks or external logins are required.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise pixel.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Information and Software Technology","success_criteria":["I can use pixel with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify grid and explain why it matters.","I can practise cell independently on a new prompt.","I can notice count needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 7, and off cells are 16 - 7 = 9, with the check 7 + 9 = 16. The shape is an L because the left column and the bottom row are on. Counting only the first row, or saying 4 by 4 means 8 cells, does not match the grid.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCount a 4 by 4 border that is all on, except the four centre cells are off. How many cells are on, how many are off, and what is the total?\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on. The four centre cells are off, and 12 + 4 = 16, which matches 4 x 4. The border is a hollow square, not a filled square, because those four middle cells stay off.","explain":"A digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells. The L turns on 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 cells, which is 7.","caption":"Count every cell in the grid, then count the on cells by row so the shape has a checkable total.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Whole grid</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">4 by 4 equals 16 cells</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">L shape on</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1+1+1+4 equals 7 on</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 7, and off cells are 16 - 7 = 9, with the check 7 + 9 = 16. The shape is an L because the left column and the bottom row are on. Counting only the first row, or saying 4 by 4 means 8 cells, does not match the grid. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Off cells are 16 minus 7, which is 9. The check is 7 on plus 9 off equals 16.","caption":"On and off counts must add to every cell in the 4 by 4 grid.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Off cells</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">16 minus 7 equals 9 off</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Full check</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">7 on plus 9 off is 16</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells. Here is a more productive model: A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 7, and off cells are 16 - 7 = 9, with the check 7 + 9 = 16. The shape is an L because the left column and the bottom row are on. Counting only the first row, or saying 4 by 4 means 8 cells, does not match the grid.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe border rows are 4, then 2, then 2, then 4 on cells, so 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 12 cells are on. The four centre cells are off, and 12 + 4 = 16, which matches 4 x 4. The border is a hollow square, not a filled square, because those four middle cells stay off.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. A common mistake is to count a 4 by 4 grid as 8 cells, or to count only the first row of on cells. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about pixel?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA digital image is a grid of pixels, and each cell is either on or off in this black-and-white example. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 7: Digital basics and data","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Digital basics and data","week":5,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells, and this L is on, off, off, off for three rows and then on, on, on, on. On cells by row are 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 7, and off cells are 16 - 7 = 9, with the check 7 + 9 = 16. The shape is an L because the left column and the bottom row are on. Counting only the first row, or saying 4 by 4 means 8 cells, does not match the grid.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A 4 by 4 grid has 16 cells. The L turns on 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 cells, which is 7.","caption":"Count every cell in the grid, then count the on cells by row so the shape has a checkable total.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14